      "items": [
        {
          "id": "engine-ticking-noise-faqs",
          "title": "Engine ticking noise FAQs",
          "description": null,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "faq",
              "question": "How much does engine ticking diagnosis cost in the UK?",
              "answer": "£50-£150 at UK independent garages."
            },
            {
              "type": "faq",
              "question": "Why does my engine tick on cold start only?",
              "answer": "Hydraulic lifters bleed down when the engine is off. Oil pressure builds within seconds of starting, silencing the tick."
            },
            {
              "type": "faq",
              "question": "Can low oil cause ticking?",
              "answer": "Yes. Low oil means insufficient pressure to fill hydraulic lifters. Top up or change the oil."
            },
            {
              "type": "faq",
              "question": "When should I worry about engine ticking?",
              "answer": "If it persists after the engine is warm, gets louder, or is accompanied by warning lights, you should get it checked immediately."
            }
          ],
          "links": [],
          "source": {
            "sectionId": "engine-ticking-noise-faqs",
            "order": 9
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "related": {
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "most-ticking-is-oil-related-but-don-t-ignore-the-rest",
          "title": "Most ticking is oil-related - but don't ignore the rest",
          "description": "Ticking noises are the most common engine complaint in our 24,650 quote dataset - and also the most misdiagnosed.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "60% of ticking cases are caused by low oil level or degraded oil, not mechanical failure. A simple oil top-up or change solves the issue for most drivers."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "However, persistent ticking after an oil change indicates valve train wear - hydraulic lifters, camshafts, or rocker arms."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The critical distinction:"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cold-start ticking that disappears within seconds = hydraulic lifters (harmless) Ticking that persists or gets worse with RPM = requires professional diagnosis"
            },
            {
              "type": "label",
              "text": "What people think vs reality"
            }
          ],
          "links": [],
          "source": {
            "sectionId": "most-ticking-is-oil-related-but-don-t-ignore-the-rest",
            "order": 1
          }
        }
      ]
    },
